2. **`handleQuote`** - Could be more precise (e.g., `computeQuotePremium`). Minor candidate.\n\n**Naming decision**: `handleQuote` could be renamed to `computeQuotePremium` for better intent revelation, and `percentOf` to `calcPercent` or keep as-is. The naming is generally good. The clearer candidate is **`handleQuote` → `computeQuotePremium`**.\n\n## Step 2: Calculate Initial APP Mass\n\nLet me calculate the mass systematically:\n\n**Constants (mass 1 each):**\n- `0` (yearsWithMHPCO check), `2` (loyalty threshold), `1` (follow-up threshold), `1` (quoteCount comparison), `0` (initial values) = ~20 occurrences throughout the file\n- String literals for error messages\n- All the numeric values in the constants section\n\nLet me count precisely by scanning the code:\n\n```\nL-constant literals: PROCESSING_FEE(5), VALID_ITEM_TYPES definition = 0, FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_PCT(10), LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_PCT(20), FOLLOW_UP_DISCOUNT_PCT(15), CURSED_SURCHARGE_PCT(50), HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_SURCHARGE_PCT(30), HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD(5), DEDUCTIBLE(100), BASE_PREMIUM values(6 numbers), INSURANCE_VALUE values(6 numbers), BLOCK_PRICING_TYPES set values\nLiteral numbers/strings in code: 0 in empty check, 2 in loyalty, 1 in follow-up, 8 in enchantment, \"dragon\", 50, 100 in claim — about 15 more\nTotal constants ≈ 40\n```\n\nLet me be more precise. I'll count by category:\n\n**Constants** (~45):\n- `0` (empty check L62), `2` (loyalty L66), `1` (followup L69), `8` (enchantment L87), `50` and `100` (L87), `\"dragon\"` (L90), `0` (deductible floor L93), `0` (mods init L98), `0` (total init L110), `0` (percentOf divider) = ~11 inline literals\n- Defined constants at file level: `5, 10, 20, 15, 50, 30, 5, 100` = 8 values\n- `BASE_PREMIUM` record: 6 values\n- `INSURANCE_VALUE`: 6 values\n- Set entries: \"rune\", \"moonstone\" = 2\n- Error message strings: ~5\n- Block pricing `3`, `60` = 2\n\nTotal constants ≈ 40\n\n**Bindings** (parameters/variables):\n- processScenario: scenario, policies, results, quoteCount, step, insuranceSum, cap, policy, payout = ~12\n- handleQuote: step, quoteIndex, yearsWithMHPCO, items, basePremium, itemModifiers, policyModifiers, rawTotal, premium = 9\n- computeClaimPayout: step, policy, totalPayout, damage, insuranceValue, damageAmount, policyItem, reimbursement, payoutForItem, actualPayout = 10\n- computeItemModifiers: items, mods, item = 3\n- computeBasePremium: items, componentCounts, total, item, type, count = 6\n- getUnitPrice: itemType = 1\n- getInsuranceValue: itemType = 1\n- validateClaim: step, policies, policy, coveredTypes, insuredCounts, item, damageCounts, damage, type, count, insuredCount = 11\n- validateItemTypes: scenario, step, types, items = 4\n- checkAllItemTypesValid: types, type = 2\n- percentOf: amount, pct = 2\n\nTotal bindings: ~51\n\n**Invocations** (function calls, mass 2):\n- reduce, forEach, map in loops\n- Function calls: processScenario (internal), handleQuote, validateClaim, computeClaimPayout, computeItemModifiers, computeBasePremium, getUnitPrice, getInsuranceValue, validateItemTypes, checkAllItemTypesValid, percentOf, Math.min, Math.max, Math.floor, Math.ceil\n- `.find()`, `.set()`, `.get()`, `.has()`, `.map()`, `.keys()`\n- Many calls to getInsuranceValue, getUnitPrice, percentOf, etc.\n\nLet me count invocation sites:\n- processScenario: validateItemTypes(1), .reduce(1), .push(3), getInsuranceValue(1), handleQuote(1), validateClaim(1), computeClaimPayout(1), policies.push(1), ... ≈ 18\n- handleQuote: computeBasePremium(1), computeItemModifiers(1), percentOf(3), Math.ceil(1) = 6\n- computeClaimPayout: getInsuranceValue(1 per damage), .find(1), Math.floor(1), Math.max(1), Math.min(1), Math.floor(1) = ~6+ per damage\n- computeBasePremium: BLOCK_PRICING_TYPES.has(1 per item), .get()(2 per component), .set(), getUnitPrice = ~7\n- computeItemModifiers: percentOf(2), getUnitPrice(2) = 4\n- validateClaim: .map(1), Map.get/set (many), .has(1), ... ≈ 10\n- validateItemTypes: .map(2), checkAllItemTypesValid(3) = 5\n- checkAllItemTypesValid: VALID_ITEM_TYPES.has(1) = 1\n- percentOf: * 1, /1 = 2\n\nTotal invocations: ~65\n\n**Conditionals** (×4):\n- if (step.op === \"quote\") [×2], if (step.op === \"claim\") = 2\n- if (items.length === 0) = 1\n- if (yearsWithMHPCO >= 2) = 1\n- if (quoteIndex > 1) = 1\n- if (component type check in block pricing): if is component + if count === 3 = 2\n- if (item.cursed) = 1\n- if (item.enchantment >= 5) = 1\n- if (policyItem && policyItem.enchantment >= 8) → 3 conditionals (truthy check + comparison)\n- else if (policyItem && policyItem.material === \"dragon\") = 2\n- if (damage.amount < 0) = 1\n- if (!coveredTypes.has) = 1\n- if (count > insuredCount) = 1\n- if (!VALID_ITEM_TYPES.has) = 1\n- forEach loops × many = see loops\n\nAnd the ternary: `(componentCounts.get(item.type) || 0)` = 1 conditional\n\nTotal conditionals: ~20\n\n**Loops** (×5):\n- processScenario: `for (const step of scenario.steps)` = 1\n- computeBasePremium: `for (const item of items)` + `for (const [type, count] of componentCounts)` = 2\n- computeItemModifiers: `for (const item of items)` = 1\n- computeClaimPayout: `for (const damage of step.incident.damages)` = 1\n- validateClaim: `for (const item of policy.items)` = 1, `for (const damage of step.incident.damages)` = 1, `for (const [type, count] of damageCounts)` = 1\n- validateItemTypes: `for (const step of scenario.steps)` = 1\n- checkAllItemTypesValid: `for (const type of types)` = 1\n\nTotal loops: ~9\n\n**Assignments** (×6):\n- processScenario: `let quoteCount = 0`, various `policies[step.policy] = {...}` = ~5\n- computeClaimPayout: `let totalPayout = 0`, `totalPayout += payoutForItem` = 2\n- computeItemModifiers: `let mods = 0`, `mods += percentOf(...)` ×2 = 3\n- computeBasePremium: `let total = 0`, `total +=`, `total +=` = 3\n- validateClaim: `const` assignments are bindings, not counting\n- destructuring: damage amount = assignment-like\n\nHmm, `const` declarations are bindings not assignments. Assignments are mutations (re-assignments):\n- `policies[step.policy] = {...}` → 1\n- `componentCounts.set(...)` → ~many (map mutations)\n- `damageCounts.set(...)` → many\n\nActually `const` with `=` is initialization, not re-assignment. The `+= ` forms are assignments. Let me count:\n- `let quoteCount = 0` → initialization, not full assignment\n- `policies[step.policy] = {...policy, remainingCap: ...}` → 1\n- `let totalPayout = 0` → initialization\n- `totalPayout += payoutForItem` → 1\n- In computeBasePremium: `let total = 0` → init; `total += getUnitPrice(...)` → 1; `total += 60` → 1; `total += count * getUnitPriceBrown(...)` → 1\n\nTotal assignments ≈ 5\n\n### Mass Calculation Summary:\n| Category | Count | Weight | Mass |\n|----------|-------|--------|------|\n| Constants | 40 | 1 | 40 |\n| Bindings | 51 | 1 | 51 |\n| Invocations | 65 | 2 | 130 |\n| Conditionals | 7 | 4 | 28 |\n| Loops | 9 | 5 | 45 |\n| Assignments | 5 | 6 | 30 |\n\n**Total Mass: ~324**\n\n## Step 3: Apply Simple Design Rules\n\n### Rule 2: Reveals Intent\n\nLooking at the code:\n\n1. **`computeClaimPayout`** - There's a subtle logic issue: the high-enchantment ≥8 check (`if (enchantment >= 8)`) takes precedence and applies 50% floor, and the `else if` for dragon material is never reached when enchantment ≥ 8. But the test \"dragon-material sword, enchantment 8, damage 1000 G → payout 400 G (enchantment clause applies)\" confirms the high-enchantment clause takes priority. This logic is correct but the comment says \"// full reimbursement (dragon material)\" for the else-if body which is misleading - there's no special dragon bonus in that branch, it just keeps the default full reimbursement. This is a clarity issue.\n\n2. **Dead code clarity issue**: The dragon material `else if` branch only applies when enchantment < 8 AND material is dragon. But in that branch, `reimbursement` already equals `damageAmount` (default), so the body is functionally empty. This is confusing. The intent of \"full reimbursement for dragon material\" is just the default behavior + a no-op branch.\n\n3. **Constant organization**: Constants are scattered - some at the top (type aliases, interfaces), some at the bottom. Would be cleaner with a logical grouping.\n\n4. **`processScenario`** mixes validation, policy tracking, quote computation, and claim computation. It's the orchestrator, which is fine, but the mutation of `policies` array via index assignment feels fragile.\n\n### Rule 3: No Duplication\n\n1. **`reduce` vs `for...of` iteration**: `getInsuranceValue` uses `.reduce` in `processScenario`, while `getUnitPrice` summing uses `for...of` in `computeBasePremium`. Pattern inconsistency.\n\n2. **`$BASE_PREMIUM` and `INSURANCE_VALUE` maps**: Both map item types to numbers. They share the same key set. This is conceptual duplication - both represent item properties.\n\n3. **Validation loops**: `validateItemTypes` iterates all steps, then `checkAllItemTypesValid` iterates types. Both have a `for...of` with a `.has` check on `VALID_ITEM_TYPES`. The calling code in `validateItemTypes` calls `checkAllItemTypesValid` for quotes and claims separately, which is correct but there are two calls with `.map(i => i.type)`.\n\n4. **Two counting loops in `validateClaim`**: `insuredCounts` and `damageCounts` are both built with `Map` + `for...of`. This is consistent but could be extracted into a helper.\n\n### Rule 4: Fewest Elements\n\n1. **`BLOCKORICING_TYPES` set**: Only contains \"rune\" and \"moonstone\". The check `BLOCK_PRICING_TYPES.has(item.type)` is used in `computeBasePremium`. Could inline the check but a named constant is clearer. ✅ Keep.\n\n2. **The `insuranceValue` function in `processScenario`**: Used only once (L44: `step.items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + getInsuranceValue(item.type), 0)`). The function `getInsuranceValue` is used in only one more place (`computeClaimPayout`). The reduce in `processScenario` could be replaced with a sum using `for...of` like `computeBasePremium`, making it consistent. But the reduce is fine.\n\n3. **The `processScenario` passing of `item.extraArgs`**: The item type validation maps `.type` using `.map` in `validateItemTypes` - this creates intermediate arrays.\n\nLet me focus on the highest impact improvements:\n\n## Step 4: Implement Refactoring\n\nThe biggest improvement I can make is **improving the clarity of `computeClaimPayout`**, which has the confusing empty `else-if` body for dragon material. Let me restructure it to clearly express the reimbursement rules:\n\n1. **Rename `handleQuote` → `computeQuotePremium`** for better intent reveal.\n2. **Clarify `computeClaimPayout` logic** with better structure.\n\nLet me also look at potential structural improvements. The most impactful single refactoring would be to clarify the claim payout computation, which currently has a confusing empty `else if` branch.\n\nLet me do the refactoring"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_23c9ee5239394dd489ec336a","name":"edit","arguments":{"path":"src/claim-office.ts","edits":[{"oldText":"function computeClaimPayout(step: ClaimStep, policy: Policy): number {\n  let totalPayout = 0;\n  for (const damage of step.incident.damages) {\n    const insuranceValue = getInsuranceValue(damage.itemType);\n    const damageAmount = damage.amount;\n    // Find matching item from policy for material/enchantment info\n    const policyItem = policy.items.find(i => i.type === damage.itemType);\n    let reimbursement = damageAmount;\n    // Apply special clauses\n    if (policyItem && policyItem.enchantment !== undefined && policyItem.enchantment >= 8) {\n      reimbursement = Math.floor(damageAmount * 50 / 100);  // 50% reimbursement\n    } else if (policyItem && policyItem.material === \"dragon\") {\n      // full reimbursement (dragon material)\n    }\n    // Apply deductible\n    const payoutForItem = Math.max(0, reimbursement - DEDUCTIBLE);\n    totalPayout += payoutForItem;\n  }\n  // Cap at remainingCap\n  const actualPayout = Math.min(totalPayout, policy.remainingCap);\n  return Math.floor(actualPayout); // round down in MHPCO's favor for payouts\n}","newText":"function computeClaimPayoutForDamage(damage: { itemType: string; amount: number }, policy: Policy): number {\n  const policyItem = policy.items.find(i => i.type === damage.itemType);\n  const reimbursement = computeReimbursementRate(damage.amount, policyItem);\n  return Math.max(0, reimbursement - DEDUCTIBLE);\n}\n\nfunction computeReimbursementRate(damageAmount :  number, policyItem: Item | undefined): number {\n  if (policyItem && policyItem.enchantment !== undefined && policyItem.enchantment >= 8) {\n    return Math.floor(damageAmount * 50 / 100);\n  }\n  return damageAmount;\n}\n\nfunction computeClaimPayout(step: ClaimStep, policy: Policy): number {\n  const totalPayout = step.incident.damages.reduce(\n    (sum, damage) => sum + computeClaimPayoutForDamage(damage, policy), 0\n  );\n  const actualPayout = Math.min(totalPayout, policy.remainingCap);\n  return Math.floor(actualPayout);\n}"},{"oldText":"function handleQuote(step: QuoteStep, quoteIndex: number, yearsWithMHPCO: number): StepResult {","newText":"function computeQuotePremium(step: QuoteStep, quoteIndex: number, yearsWithMHPCO: number): StepResult {"},{"oldText":"      results.push(handleQuote(step, quoteCount, scenario.customer.yearsWithMHPCO));","newText":"      results.push(computeQuotePremium(step, quoteCount, scenario.customer.yearsWithMHPCO));"}]}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/deepseek-v4-pro","usage":{"input":386,"output":4228,"cacheRead":14848,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":19462,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784949462303,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-975178c2-b272-40b1-a76d-11a28b935162","responseModel":"deepseek/deepseek-v4-pro"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_23c9ee5239394dd489ec336a","toolName":"edit","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Successf