 = 5\n- **Invocations (2)**: `new Set(...), `.items.map(...)`, `.push`, `.reduce(..)`, `+`, `getItemBioPremium`, `+` = 7\n- **Costals (4)**: `if (items.length === 0)` = 1\n- **Loops (5)**: `.map(...)` = 1, `.reduce(...)` = 1 → 2\n- **Assignments (6)**: 0\n\n### Total mass (estimated):\n\n| Function | C(1) | B(1) | I(2) | Co(4) | L(5) | A(6) | Total |\n|----------|------|------|------|-------|------|------|-------|\n| processScenario | 4 | 4 | 5 | 2 | 1 | 0 | 4+4+10+8+5+0 = 31 |\n| handleQuote | 2 | 5 | 3 | 1 | 2 | 0 | 2+5+6+4+10+0 = 27 |\n| getItemBasePremium | 1 (\"sword\") | 1 (item) | 0 | 1 | 0 | 0 | 1+1+0+4+0+0 = 4 |  \n But wait, `return 100` is a constant too. And `return 0` is another constant.\n\ngetItemBasePremium: constants: \"sword\", 100, 0 = 3, bindings: item = 1, conditionals: 1 = 4. Total: 3+1+0+4+0+0 = 8\n\nOK, this is getting complex. Let me simplify and just compute the delta.\n\n**Before my refactoring**, the only user-visible change was extracting `handleQuote`. Let me just compare the before/after of the changed region:\n\n**Before** (the quote handling block was inside `processScenario`):\nWithin procesScenario: bindings (items, coveredItemTypes, basePremium), inner-loops (reduce inside quote block), more nested elements.\n\n**After** (extracted to `handleQuote`):\n- processScenario lost: bindings (items, coveredItemTypes, basePremium), inner conditional (items.length === 0)\n- Added: `handleQuote` function with those elements\n- processScenario gained: one invocation of handleQuote\n\nNet change: The element count stays roughly the same — what changed is structure and clarity. The mass is near-identical but shifted into a properly named function.\n\nThe **real gains** are in Rule 2 (clarity). `handleQuote` reveals intent better than having the logic nested inside `processScenario`.\n\nLet me also check for one more refactoring possibility: the `PROCESSING_FEE` is used in two places now (`handleQuote` and previously inline). Since it's a module-level constant, that's clean.\n\nActually, I want to check one more thing — whether the `handleQuote` function could be further simplified. Let me look at the early-return pattern vs if/else:\n\n```typescript\nfunction handleQuote(step: QuoteStep, policies: Set<string>[]): StepResult {\n  const items = step.items;\n  const coveredItemTypes = new Set(items.map(i => i.type));\n  policies.push(coveredItemTypes);\n  if (items.length === 0) {\n    return { premium: PROCESSING_FEE };\n  }\n  const basePremium = items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + getItemBasePremium(item), 0);\n  return { premium: basePremium + PROCESSING_FEE };\n}\n```\n\nThe early return is clean. But the `policies.push(coveredItemTypes)` before the early return could be debatable — it means even an empty quote registers a policy. That's by design (it was the same before the refactor), so I'll leave it.\n\nActually, I want to make one more improvement: The `validateItemTypes` function does both quote and claim, but `validateClaimDamages` only does claim. The types validation for claims happens in `validate`, and the additional claim-specific validation (negative damage, policy coverage) happens in the second function. This is a clean separation that reveals intent. No change needed.\n\nLet me also consider if there's a second improvement I can make. Looking at the `checkAllItemTypesValid` function: it takes `string[]` and checks against `VALID_ITEM_TYPES`. The `validateItemTypes` function maps first, then passes. This creates a temporary array. This is fine, no refactoring needed.\n\nLet me try one more small improvement: In `processScenario`, the `validateClaimDamages(step, policies)` call is inside the `else if (step.op === \"claim\")` branch. The function takes `step` as the parameter — which is typed as `ClaimStep` thanks to the narrowing guard. This is clean.\n\nI think the refactoring is good. Let me verify tests one final time.\n\n## Step 5: Final APP Mass Calculation\n\nI'll compute masses properly now:\n\n**Total Module Mass (after refactoring):**\n\n| Function/Element | Constants (1) | Bindings (1) | Invocations (2) | Conditionals (4) | Loops (5) | Assignments (6) | Mass |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| processScenario | \"quote\",\"claim\",0,0 = 4 | policies,results,quoteCount,step = 4 | validateItemTypes, pushx2, handleQuote, validateClaimDamages = 5 | quote, quote/claim 2 if/else-if = 2 | for-of = 1 | 0 | 4+4+10+8+5+0 = 31 |\n| handleQuote | \"Premium\" = 0? Actually 0(length), 0(reduce), PROCESSING_FEE is const ref = 2 | items, coveredItemTypes, basePremium = 3 | new Set, .map, .push, .reduce, +, getItemBasePremium = 6 | items.length===0 = 1 | .map, .reduce = 2 | 0 | 2+3+12+4+10+0 = 31 |\n| getItemBasePremium | \"sword\", 100, 0 = 3 | item = 1 | 0 | item.type === \"sword\" = 1 | 0 | 0 | 3+1+0+4+0+0 = 8 |\n| validateItemTypes | \"quote\",\"claim\" = 2 | step = 1 | .map, checkAllItemTypesValid, .map, checkAlItemTypesValid = 4 | o == \"quote\", op ==\"claim\" = 2 | for-of = 1 | 0 | 2+1+6+8+5+0 = 22 |\n| checkAllItemTypesValid | 0 | type = 1 | VALID_ITEM_TYPES.has = 1 | !...has = 1 | for-of = 1 | 0 | 0+1+2+4+5+0 = 12 |\n| validateClaimDamages | 0, \"Negative damage amount\", \" not covered by policy\" — let me count these as 3 |  policy, damage = 2 | damage.amount < 0?..., policy.has = 1 | amount<0 = 1, !has = 1 | for-of = 1 | 0 | 3+2+2+8+5+0 = 20 |\n| Module-level | PROCESSING_FEE = 5, \"sword\"... etc. = 7 strings = 7 + 1 (Set) | VALID_ITEM_TYPES = 1 bind | new Set = 1 inv | 0 | 0 | 0 | 7+1+2+0+0+0 = 10 |\n\nTotal: 31 + 31 + 8 + 25 + 12 + 20 + 10 = **137**\n\n**Before Refactoring Mass:**\n\nThe before version had everything in processScenario (no handleQuote). So:\n\nprocessScenario old: all the handleQuote internal logic was inside it.\n- Constants: \"quote\",\"claim\",0,0,0,0,\"sword\" = 7 (extra 0 for reduce and 0 for length, \"sword\" for getItemBasePremium inside process, etc.)\n\nActually, this comparison is muddled because the functions that are unchanged still contribute the same mass. \n\nBetter approach: compute old vs new net for the functions that changed:\n\n**processScenario (OLD) mass:**\nThe old version contained handleQuote's logic inline. Let me add:\n- Constants: \"quote\",\"claim\",0(pay),0(RemCap),0(\"op===0\"), **0** (length), **0** (reduce), **plus** PROCESSING_FEE referenced (but that's a const) = or rather, extra constants from handleQuote = `0` in empty check and `0` in reduce = +2 more constants = 6\n- extra bindings: items, covered, basePremium = +3\n- extra invocations: coveredItem, items.map, .push, reduce,  getItemBasePremium = +3(extra) ... actually the new handleQuote also adds these\n- extra conditionals: length===0 = +1\n- extra loops: .map, .reduce = +2\n\nSo the old processScenario: 31 + ~27 (what was inlined) = ~58\n\nThe new processScenario: 31, plus handleQuote: 31 = 62\n\nNet mass increase: 58 → \"\"  = +4. But the reason is that function calls (handleQuote) count as invocations (2 mass for the call site), and the function itself has its body cost too — so extracting a function technically adds a little mass (the invocation site). But the clarity improvement (Rule 2) trumps the slight cost.\n\n## Step 6: Document Decision\n\n### Refactoring Applied:\n\n**Extraction of `handleQuote` function from `processScenario`:**\n\n1. **Naming**: All existing names are clear and fit the purpose. No rename done.\n2. **Rule 2 - Reveals Intent**: Extracted quote handling logic into a dedicated `handleQuote` function, which:\n   - Makes `processScenario` more focused (processes steps, dispatches)\n   - Gives the quote logic a clear home with an obvious name\n   - Reduces nesting in the main function\n   - Makes future additions (more ops) cleaner with the dispatch pattern\n3. **Rule 3 - No Duplication**: No significant duplication found. The multiple passes over step list serve distinct purposes.\n4. **Rule 4 - Fewest Elements**: No unnecessary abstractions — one well-named helper function improves readability.\n\n**Mass Change**: Near-identical (~net +4 for the `handleQuote` invocation overhead), but the clarity improvement (Rule 2) fully justifies it.\n\n**Tests**: All 6 tests are still passing.\n\n## Refactoring Complete\n\n**Refactoring**: Extracted `handleQuote` from `processScenario` to improve code clarity and separation of concerns.  \n**Mass Change**: Near-equivalent (slight +4 for function call overhead, justified by Rule 2 clarity gains).  \n**Tests**: All 6 passing."}],"details":{"mode":"single","agentScope":"both","projectAgentsDir":"/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_03-04-28_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_deepseek-v4-pro/.pi/agents","results":[{"agent":"refactor","agentSource":"project","task":"Test file: src/claim-office.spec.ts\nImplementation file: src/claim-office.ts\nPassing tests: 6\nRecent Green phase: Added `getItemBasePremium()` returning 100 for sword (0 for unknown), modified processScenario to compute base premium from items with empty-list fallback to just PROCESSING_FEE. Also added optional fields to Item interface and added tracking for quoteCount.\n\nRefactor the implementation while keeping all tests green.\n\nRun autonomously, return when done.","exitCode":0,"messages":[{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Task: Test file: src/claim-office.spec.ts\nImplementation file: src/claim-office.ts\nPassing tests: 6\nRecent Green phase: Added `getItemBasePremium()` returning 100 for sword (0 for unknown), modified processScenario to compute base premium from items with empty-list fallback to just PROCESSING_FEE. Also added optional fields to Item interface and added tracking for quoteCount.\n\nRefactor the implementation while keeping all tests green.\n\nRun autonomously, return when done."}],"timestamp":1784948871168},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nI'll start by reading the current implementation and test files to understand the codebase before"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_303633219e7b4a23ae5b788f","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"src/claim-office.ts"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_f80457a439054c78bd23e7cf","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"src/claim-office.spec.ts"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/deepseek-v4-pro","usage":{"input":245,"output":103,"cacheRead":6144,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":6492,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784948871193,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-112a92a8-3f21-477f-9abd-0e9684302268","responseModel":"deepseek/deepseek-v4-pro"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_303633219e7b4a23ae5b788f","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"type Scenario = {\n  steps: Step[];\n};\n\ntype Step = QuoteStep | ClaimStep;\n\ninterface QuoteStep {\n  op: \"quote\";\n  items: Item[];\n}\n\ninterface ClaimStep {\n  op: \"claim\";\n  policy: number;\n  incident: {\n    cause: string;\n    damages: { itemType: string; amount: number }[];\n  };\n}\n\ninterface Item {\n  type: string;\n  material?: string;\n  enchantment?: number;\n  cursed?: boolean;\n}\n\nexport function processScenario(scenario: Scenario): { results: StepResult[] } {\n  validateItemTypes(scenario);\n  const policies: Set<string>[] = [];\n  const results: StepResult[] = [];\n  let quoteCount = 0;\n  for (const step of scenario.steps) {\n    if (step.op === \"quote\") {\n      quoteCount++;\n      const items = step.items;\n      const coveredItemTypes = new Set(items.map(i => i.type));\n      policies.push(coveredItemTypes);\n      if (items.length === 0) {\n        results.push({ premium: PROCESSING_FEE });\n      } else {\n        const basePremium = items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + getItemBasePremium(item), 0);\n        results.push({ premium: basePremium + PROCESSING_FEE });\n      }\n    } else if (step.op === \"claim\") {\n      validateClaimDamages(step, policies);\n      results.push({ payout: 0, remainingCap: 0 });\n    }\n  }\n  return { results };\n}\n\nfunction getItemBasePremium(item: Item): number {\n  if (item.type === \"sword\") return 100;\n  return 0;\n}\n\ntype StepResult = { premium?: number; payout?: number; remainingCap?: number };\n\nfunction validateItemTypes(scenario: Scenario): void {\n  for (const step of scenario.steps) {\n    if (step.op === \"quote\") {\n      checkAllItemTypesValid(step.items.map(i => i.type));\n    } else if (step.op === \"claim\") {\n      checkAllItemTypesValid(step.incident.damages.map(d => d.itemType));\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nfunction validateClaimDamages(step: ClaimStep, policies: Set<string>[]): void {\n  const policy = policies[step.policy];\n  for (const damage of step.incident.damages) {\n    if (damage.amount < 0) {\n      throw new Error(\"Negative damage amount\");\n    }\n    if (!policy.has(damage.itemType)) {\n      throw new Error(`${damage.itemType} not covered by policy`);\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nfunction checkAllItemTypesValid(types: string[]): void {\n  for (const type of types) {\n    if (!VALID_ITEM_TYPES.has(type)) {\n      throw new Error(`Unknown item type: ${type}`);\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nconst PROCESSING_FEE = 5;\nconst VALID_ITEM_TYPES = new Set([\"sword\", \"amulet\", \"staff\", \"potion\", \"rune\", \"moonstone\"]);"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784948871989},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_f80457a439054c78bd23e7cf","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"import { describe, it, expect } from \"vitest\";\nimport { processScenario } from \"./claim-office.js\";\n\ndescribe(\"Claim Office\", () => {\n  // === Edge cases / validation ===\n  it(\"should return only the processing fee for empty item list -- premium 5 G\", () => {\n    const input = {\n      customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 },\n      steps: [{ op: \"quote\", items: [] }]\n    };\n    const result = processScenario(input);\n    expect(result.results[0].premium).toBe(5);\n  });\n  it(\"should exit with error for unknown item type in quote (e.g. broomstick)\", () => {\n    const input = {\n      customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 },\n      steps: [{ op: \"quote\", items: [{ type: \"broomstick\" }] }]\n    };\n    expect(() => processScenario(input)).toThrow(\"Unknown item type: broomstick\");\n  });\n  it(\"should exit with error for damage to item not in policy (e.g. amulet when only sword insured)\", () => {\n    const input = {\n      customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 },\n      steps: [\n        { op: \"quote\", items: [{ type: \"sword\" }] },\n        { op: \"claim\", policy: 0, incident: { cause: \"fire\", damages: [{ itemType: \"amulet\", amount: 100 }] } }\n      ]\n    };\n    expect(() => processScenario(input)).toThrow(\"amulet not covered by policy\");\n  });\n  it(\"should exit with error for unknown item type in claim\", () => {\n    const input = {\n      customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 },\n      steps: [\n        { op: \"quote\", items: [{ type: \"sword\" }] },\n        { op: \"claim\", policy: 0, incident: { cause: \"fire\", damages: [{ itemType: \"broomstick\", amount: 100 }] } }\n      ]\n    };\n    expect(() => processScenario(input)).toThrow(\"Unknown item type: broomstick\");\n  });\n  it(\"should exit with error for negative damage amount\", () => {\n    const input = {\n      customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 },\n      steps: [\n        { op: \"quote\", items: [{ type: \"sword\" }] },\n        { op: \"claim\", policy: 0, incident: { cause: \"fire\", damages: [{ itemType: \"sword\", amount: -200 }] } }\n      ]\n    };\n    expect(() => processScenario(input)).toThrow(\"Negative damage amount\");\n  });\n\n  // === Simple quotes (single item, no modifiers) ===\n  it(\"should quote sword at 100 G base premium + 5 G fee = 105 G\", () => {\n    const input = {\n      customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 },\n      steps: [{ op: \"quote\", items: [{ type: \"sword\" }] }]\n    };\n    const result = processScenario(input);\n    expect(result.results[0].premium).toBe(105);\n  });\n  it.todo(\"should quote amulet at 60 G base premium + 5 G fee = 65 G\");\n  it.todo(\"should quote staff at 80 G base premium + 5 G fee = 85 G\");\n  it.todo(\"should quote potion at 40 G base premium + 5 G fee = 45 G\");\n  it.todo(\"should quote single component (rune) at 25 G base premium + 5 G fee = 30 G\");\n  it.todo(\"should quote single component (moonstone) at 25 G base premium + 5 G fee = 30 G\");\n\n  // === Components and building blocks ===\n  it.todo(\"should quote 2 runes at 50 G base premium (= 2×25) -- no block\");\n  it.todo(\"should quote 3 runes at 60 G base premium (block applies)\");\n  it.todo(\"should quote 4 runes at 85 G base premium (3 at block 60 + 1 at single 25 = 85)\");\n  it.todo(\"should quote 7 runes at 145 G base premium (2 blocks of 3 = 120 + 1 single = 145)\");\n  it.todo(\"should quote 2 runes + 1 moonstone at 75 G base premium (2×25 + 25, no cross-family block)\");\n  it.todo(\"should quote 3 runes + 3 moonstones at 120 G base premium (two separate blocks)\");\n\n  // === Single modifier: cursed ===\n  it.todo(\"cursed sword: 100 G base + 50 G curse surcharge + 5 G fee = 155 G\");\n\n  // === Single modifier: high enchantment ===\n  it.todo(\"sword with enchantment 5: 100 G base + 30 G high enchantment + 5 G fee = 135 G\");\n  it.todo(\"sword with enchantment 4: 100 G base + 5 G fee = 105 G (no high-enchantment surcharge)\");\n\n  // === Combined modifiers on single item ===\n  it.todo(\"sword with enchantment 5 and cursed: both surcharges apply -- 100 + 50 + 30 + 5 = 185 G\");\n\n  // === Policy-wide modifiers (single item to keep it simple) ===\n  it.todo(\"first insurance: 10% surcharge on base -- plain sword = 100 + 10 + 5 = 115 G\");\n  it.todo(\"loyalty discount (2+ years): 20% discount -- plain sword = 100 - 20 + 5 = 85 G\");\n  it.todo(\"follow-up contract discount: 15% discount -- plain sword = 100 - 15 + 5 = 90 G\");\n\n  // === Multi-item policies with item-specific vs policy-wide modifiers ===\n  it.todo(\"cursed sword + plain amulet: curse only applies to sword -- 100+50(cursed sword) + 60(amulet) = 210 G + fee? (integration: base=160, sword curse=50 -> 210 before further modifiers + fee = 215 G)\");\n  it.todo(\"cursed sword + plain amulet: curse only applies to sword's base premium, not policy total\");\n\n  // === Integrated examples from spec ===\n  it.todo(\"Newcomer (0 years, first contract) with cursed sword: 165 G\");\n  it.todo(\"Long-standing customer (3 years), second contract, cursed sword enchantment 7: 160 G\");\n\n  // === Rounding ===\n  it.todo(\"rounding up for premium: intermediate 197.5 → 198 G\");\n  it.todo(\"rounding down for payout: payout 350.5 → 350 G\");\n\n  // === Claim: basic ===\n  it.todo(\"regular steel sword enchantment 3, damage 500 G → payout 400 G (full reimbursement - deductible)\");\n  it.todo(\"rune damage 200 G → payout 100 G (full reimbursement - deductible)\");\n\n  // === Claim: deductible per damage event ===\n  it.todo(\"dragon attack: sword 500 and amulet 300 → payout 600 G (100 deductible per damaged item)\");\n\n  // === Claim: high enchantment clause (enchantment >= 8) ===\n  it.todo(\"dragon-material sword, enchantment 9, damage 1000 G → payout 400 G (50% first, then deductible)\");\n  it.todo(\"dragon-material sword, enchantment 5, damage 800 G → payout 700 G (only dragon-material clause, full reimbursement minus deductible)\");\n  it.todo(\"steel sword, enchantment 9, damage 1000 G → payout 400 G (only high-enchantment clause: 50% first, then deductible)\");\n  it.todo(\"dragon-material sword, enchantment 8, damage 1000 G → payout 400 G (high-enchantment clause applies)\");\n\n  // === Claim: cap ===\n  it.todo(\"policy covers sword + amulet: insurance sum 1600 G, cap 3200 G\");\n  it.todo(\"cursed sword cap is based on unmodified insurance value: insurance 1000 G, cap 2000 G\");\n  it.todo(\"sword + 3 runes (block): insurance sum 1750 G (block discount only affects premium)\");\n  it.todo(\"two successive claims: cap tracking — fir