servation**: The `processScenario` function has this pattern for each step: it dispatches based on `step.op`. The quote-handling body has grown. I'll extract the quote-handling into a `handleQuote` function (Rule 2 — better reveals intent, Rule 4 — reduces nesting).\n\nLet me also look at a small improvement: the `else` block after `if (step.op === \"quote\")` has two conditions (`else if` for claim and potentially nothing for else). Having an `else if` only for `\"claim\"` is fine but the function will grow with more operations.\n\n**Planned refactoring**: Extract `handleQuote` and `handleClaim` helper functions from `processScenario` to reduce nesting and improve readability. This follows Rule 2 (better reveals intent) and Rule 4 (fewer elements in each function scope).\n\nLet me implement this:\n\n## Step 4: Implement Refact"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_cfd71732669a46d4abd371fa","name":"edit","arguments":{"path":"src/claim-office.ts","edits":[{"oldText":"export function processScenario(scenario: Scenario): { results: StepResult[] } {\n  validateItemTypes(scenario);\n  const policies: Set<string>[] = [];\n  const results: StepResult[] = [];\n  let quoteCount = 0;\n  for (const step of scenario.steps) {\n    if (step.op === \"quote\") {\n      quoteCount++;\n      const items = step.items;\n      const coveredItemTypes = new Set(items.map(i => i.type));\n      policies.push(coveredItemTypes);\n      if (items.length === 0) {\n        results.push({ premium: PROCESSING_FEE });\n      } else {\n        const basePremium = items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + getItemBasePremium(item), 0);\n        results.push({ premium: basePremium + PROCESSING_FEE });\n      }\n    } else if (step.op === \"claim\") {\n      validateClaimDamages(step, policies);\n      results.push({ payout: 0, remainingCap: 0 });\n    }\n  }\n  return { results };\n}","newText":"export function processScenario(scenario: Scenario): { results: StepResult[] } {\n  validateItemTypes(scenario);\n  const 