\nAssignments:\n- results.push() — push is an invocation not assignment, but mutation of array\n- The code doesn't have explicit assignment operators except:\n  - None obvious. push is mutation but counted as invocation.\n\nLet me recount more carefully:\n- results: [] initializer = binding (already counted)\n- policies: [] initializer = binding\n- quoteCount = 0 = binding\n- coveredItemTypes = new Set(...) = binding\n- basePremium = ... = binding\n\nNo reassignments using =. push() is an invocation, not an assignment.\n\nTotal Mass: 19 + 20 + 44 + 32 + 25 + 0 = 140\n\nActually wait, let me redo this more carefully. Let me think about what counts as what.\n\nActually, I'll simplify the mass calculation. Let me just focus on what can be improved rather than getting bogged down in exact mass numbers. The key issue I see:\n\n```\n\nLet me now check for the \"reveals intent\" issues and potential simplifications.\n\n## Step 3: Apply Simple Design Rules\n\n### Rule 1: Tests Pass ✅\nAll 7 tests passing.\n\n### Rule 2: Reveals Intent\n\nLooking at the code, I see one clear improvement opportunity in `getItemBasePremium`:\n\n```typescript\nfunction getItemBasePremium(item: Item): number {\n  if (item.type === \"sword\") return 100;\n  if (item.type === \"amulet\") return 60;\n  return 0;\n}\n```\n\nThis is a lookup pattern. Using a map/object lookup would better reveal intent (this is a mapping from item type to base premium) and more importantly, implementing a lookup will make **future item types easier** since the test list shows staff (80), potion (40) are coming next.\n\n**Assessment**: The current if-chain pattern is OK with 2 items, but we already know more are coming. A `Record<string, number>` lookup would reveal intent better: \"these are the base premiums by item type.\"\n\n### Rule 3: No Duplication (DRY)\n\nLooking at `handleQuote`:\n```typescript\nconst coveredItemTypes = new Set(items.map(i => i.type));\n```\n\nThis creates a Set by mapping item types. This pattern is used only once — no duplication currently.\n\n### Rule 4: Fewest Elements\n\nThe `getItemBasePremium` function currently uses two conditionals (mass: 8) instead of a lookup (which could be a single constant lookup). A lookup map: `BASE_PREMIUMS[item.type] ?? 0` — that's 1 constant (the map) + 1 invocation (property access).\n\n---\n\n## Decision: Convert `getItemBasePremium` to a lookup map\n\nThis is the clearest improvement:\n1. **Rule 2 (Reveals Intent)**: A `BASE_PREMIUMS` constant makes it crystal clear this is a static lookup of premiums by item type\n2. **Rule 4 (Fewest Elements)**: Reduces two conditionals to a single lookup expression\n3. **Better for future changes**: Next tests will add more base premiums; the lookup handles this trivially\n\nLet me implement"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_31ea4c9315f641dfb8879820","name":"edit","arguments":{"path":"src/claim-office.ts","edits":[{"oldText":"function getItemBasePremium(item: Item): number {\n  if (item.type === \"sword\") return 100;\n  if (item.type === \"amulet\") return 60;\n  return 0;\n}","newText":"const BASE_PREMIUM: Record<string, number> = {\n  sword: 100,\n  amulet: 60,\n};\n\nfunction getItemBasePremium(item: Item): number {\n  return BASE_PREMIUM[item.type] ?? 0;\n}"}]}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/deepseek-v4-pro","usage":{"input":343,"output":1840,"cacheRead":9216,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":11399,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784948922142,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-e7309f02-307d-463a-a33e-ef58b7a56882","responseModel":"deepseek/deepseek-v4-pro"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_31ea4c9315f641dfb8879820","toolName":"edit","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Successfully replaced 1 block(s) in src/claim-office.ts."}],"details":{"diff":"    ...\n 52   const basePremium = items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + getItemBasePremium(item), 0);\n 53   return { pre