ved: 1.\n\n**Total mass savingsband: ~3**\n\nThe exact mass before/after is less important than the fact that we removed a misleading wrapper function. The clarity gain (Rule 2) is the real win.\n\n## Step 6: Document Decision\n\n**Refactoring Applied:**\n- **Remove `getItemBasePremium`** — This function was a thin wrapper that called `getUnitPrice(item.type)`. It accepted an `Item` object but only used `.type`, misleading readers into thinking it might handle item-specific modifiers like `cursed`, `material`, or `enchantment`. By inlining its call into `computeBasePremium`, the code directly reveals that only the item type matters for base premium calculation at this stage.\n- **Rename `basePremium` → `total`** in `computeBasePremium` — `basePremium` was ambiguous; the function accumulates both base prices and block pricing discounts into this variable, so `total` better captures what's being built up. Both are local variables within the same function so there's no external impact.\n\n**Mass Change:**\n- `getItemBasePremium`: removed 1 binding + 1 invocation (mass -3)\n- Renamed variable: no net mass change\n- Net: very small mass reduction\n\n**Tests:** All 17 passing, 26 todos skipped.\n\n---\n\n**Refactoring Complete:**\n**Refactoring**: Removed dead-wrapper function `getItemBasePremium` (Rule 4 — fewest elements) and renamed `basePremium` → `total` for clarity (Rule 2 — reveals intent)\n**Quality gain**: Code now clearly shows that base premium calculation only depends on `item.type`, eliminating confusion about possibly unused `Item` fields (`cursed`, `material`, `enchantment`).\n**Mass Change**: Marginal reduction (removed unnecessary function)\n**Tests**: All 17 passing ✅"}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/deepseek-v4-pro","usage":{"input":1119,"output":1156,"cacheRead":14080,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":16355,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":